When an AI generates output that looks very similar to a specific training example, it does not by itself prove that the model has memorized that exact entry. Instead, such similarity often arises from learned patterns or interpolation of nearby data points. To decide whether memorization is occurring you should examine several criteria:  
(1) if the generated text matches the original phrasing word for word;  
(2) how much it changes when a single token is altered;  
(3) whether out‑of‑distribution examples that should not resemble the example also fail to match;  
(4) whether enlarging model size or changing prompts reduces similarity;  
(5) if information about memorization can be verified by checking exact training copies.
